
### tree renders siblings, nesting, and the orphan
$ git stack tree
* main (trunk)
    feat-a (needs restack: 1 behind)
      feat-b (1 commit(s))
        feat-b1 (1 commit(s))
      feat-c (1 commit(s))
    feat-x-child (parent 'feat-x' missing; run `git stack sync`)
[exit 0]

### up from the trunk offers the orphan's root alongside feat-a (issue #85)
$ git stack up
'main' has multiple children; pick one:
  git stack up feat-a
  git stack up feat-x-child
[exit 1]

### parent reports the recorded parent
$ git stack parent
feat-a
[exit 0]

### parent/track reject a cycle, a self-parent, and a missing branch
$ git stack parent feat-b1
error: 'feat-b1' is downstream of 'feat-b'; setting it as parent would create a cycle
[exit 1]
$ git stack track feat-b1
error: 'feat-b1' is downstream of 'feat-b'; tracking it would create a cycle
[exit 1]
$ git stack parent feat-b
error: a branch cannot be its own parent
[exit 1]
$ git stack parent no-such-branch
error: branch 'no-such-branch' does not exist
[exit 1]

### up with multiple children exits non-zero
$ git stack up
'feat-a' has multiple children; pick one:
  git stack up feat-b
  git stack up feat-c
[exit 1]

### restack replays the sibling subtrees onto an advanced parent
$ git stack tree
  main (trunk)
    feat-a (needs restack: 1 behind)
    * feat-b (needs restack: 1 behind)
        feat-b1 (1 commit(s))
      feat-c (needs restack: 1 behind)
    feat-x-child (parent 'feat-x' missing; run `git stack sync`)
[exit 0]
$ git stack restack
restacking stack rooted at feat-a
restacking feat-a onto main
restacking feat-b onto feat-a
restacking feat-b1 onto feat-b
restacking feat-c onto feat-a
done.
[exit 0]
$ git stack tree
  main (trunk)
    feat-a (2 commit(s))
    * feat-b (1 commit(s))
        feat-b1 (1 commit(s))
      feat-c (1 commit(s))
    feat-x-child (parent 'feat-x' missing; run `git stack sync`)
[exit 0]

### sync heals the orphan onto the trunk
$ git stack sync
syncing stack rooted at feat-x-child
'feat-x-child': parent 'feat-x' no longer exists; reparenting onto trunk 'main'
done.
[exit 0]
$ git stack tree
  main (trunk)
    feat-a (2 commit(s))
      feat-b (1 commit(s))
        feat-b1 (1 commit(s))
      feat-c (1 commit(s))
  * feat-x-child (1 commit(s))
[exit 0]
feat-x-child parent: main

### up <name> and a lone child both index the run-time-built list (issue #85)
$ git stack up
Switched to branch 'base-b'
[exit 0]
HEAD after the lone child's [0] checkout: base-b
$ git stack up base-b
Switched to branch 'base-b'
[exit 0]
HEAD after the named include? checkout: base-b

### sync recovers a branch whose parent was squash-merged and deleted
$ git stack sync
syncing stack rooted at feature-b
'feature-b': parent 'feature-a' no longer exists; reparenting onto trunk 'main'
restacking feature-b onto main
done.
[exit 0]
feature-b parent: main
feature-b behind main: 0
feature-b commits above main: 1
feature-b stackBase == main tip: yes

### a stack past the 4 KB scan boundary renders and syncs intact
branch-list scan exceeds the 4 KB backtick cap: yes
stack-config scan exceeds the 4 KB backtick cap: yes
$ git stack tree | grep zzz
    zzz-stack-bottom 
      zzz-stack-top 
[exit 0]
zzz rows in tree (expect 2): 2
$ git stack sync
syncing stack rooted at zzz-stack-bottom
done.
[exit 0]
zzz-stack-top parent after sync: zzz-stack-bottom
zzz-stack-bottom parent after sync: main

### an orphan-looking stack past the branch-list cap is not 'healed' away
branch-list scan exceeds the 4 KB backtick cap: yes
stack-config scan (small and accurate here): branch.zzz-stack-bottom.stackparent mainbranch.zzz-stack-top.stackparent zzz-stack-bottom
$ git stack tree | grep zzz
    zzz-stack-bottom 
      zzz-stack-top 
[exit 0]
zzz rows in tree (expect 2): 2
$ git stack sync
syncing stack rooted at zzz-stack-bottom
done.
[exit 0]
zzz-stack-top parent after sync: zzz-stack-bottom

### tree reads ahead/behind across batches of the chunked scan
chain rows in tree (expect 130): 130
chain rows at exactly 1 commit (expect 130): 130

### plant and fell edit the trunk list on the compiled binary
$ git stack init main
trunk set to main
[exit 0]
$ git stack plant develop
planted develop; trunk(s): main, develop
[exit 0]
$ git stack plant release
planted release; trunk(s): main, develop, release
[exit 0]
stack.trunk (run together): maindeveloprelease
$ git stack fell develop
felled develop; trunk(s): main, release
[exit 0]
stack.trunk after fell (run together): mainrelease
$ git stack plant develop
planted develop; trunk(s): main, release, develop
[exit 0]
$ git stack fell main release develop
felled main, release, develop; no trunk left -- the next command will auto-detect main
[exit 0]
stack.trunk (unset): 

### restack rewrites a branch in another worktree and skips a dirty one
$ git stack restack
restacking stack rooted at feat-a
restacking feat-b onto feat-a in worktree '<repo>-wt-b'
warning: skipping 'feat-c': its worktree '<repo>-wt-c' has uncommitted changes
error: restack skipped 1 branch(es): feat-c
Deal with the branches warned about above, then re-run 'git stack restack'.
[exit 1]
feat-b behind feat-a: 0
feat-c behind feat-a: 1

### a config lock released during the retries is waited out
$ git stack create feat-a
created feat-a on top of main
[exit 0]
feat-a parent: main

### sync follows the trunk's upstream when stack.trunkUpstream is set
$ git stack tree
  main (trunk, restacks onto origin/main)
  * feat-a (needs restack: 1 behind)
[exit 0]
$ git stack sync
fetching origin
syncing stack rooted at feat-a
restacking feat-a onto origin/main
done.
[exit 0]
feat-a behind origin/main: 0
main behind origin/main (untouched): 1

### anchors: tree, up from an anchor, and sync moving the anchor up
$ git stack up
'wt-x' has multiple stacks; pick one:
  git stack up feat-a
  git stack up fix-c
[exit 1]
$ git stack tree
  main (trunk)
    wt-x (anchor, 1 behind; run `git stack sync`)
    * feat-a (needs restack: 1 behind)
      fix-c (needs restack: 1 behind)
[exit 0]
$ git stack sync
syncing stack rooted at feat-a
restacking feat-a onto main
syncing stack rooted at fix-c
restacking fix-c onto main
fast-forwarding anchor wt-x to main
done.
[exit 0]
wt-x behind main: 0
fix-c behind main: 0

### submit pushes the stack, then refuses a lease someone else broke
$ git stack submit
submitting stack rooted at feat-a
pushed feat-a to origin (new branch)
pushed feat-b to origin (new branch)
done.
[exit 0]
$ git stack submit
submitting stack rooted at feat-a
warning: not pushing 'feat-a': 'origin/feat-a' has commits this branch does not include; fetch them and bring them in (or check they can go) first
pushed feat-b to origin
error: submit failed for 1 branch(es): feat-a
Deal with the branches warned about above, then re-run 'git stack submit'.
[exit 1]
feat-b behind origin/feat-b: 0

### mode github on a linear stack, and tree tagging its root
$ git stack mode github
stack rooted at feat-a is now github
set stack.trunkUpstream to true, so github stacks follow the trunk GitHub merges into (this applies to every stack in the repository)
2 branch(es) have no pull request yet; 'git stack submit' opens them
[exit 0]
$ git stack mode
github
[exit 0]
$ git stack tree
  main (trunk)
    feat-a (1 commit(s)) [github]
    * feat-b (1 commit(s))
[exit 0]

### github mode: submit opens and stacks pull requests, mode registers them
$ git stack mode github
stack rooted at feat-a is now github
set stack.trunkUpstream to true, so github stacks follow the trunk GitHub merges into (this applies to every stack in the repository)
1 branch(es) have no pull request yet; 'git stack submit' opens them
[exit 0]
$ git stack submit
submitting stack rooted at feat-a
pushed feat-a to origin (new branch)
pushed feat-b to origin (new branch)
pushed feat-c to origin (new branch)
opened #2 for feat-b onto feat-a
opened #3 for feat-c onto feat-b
created GitHub stack #4: #1, #2, #3
done.
[exit 0]
PR #1 feat-a -> main (open) feat-a
PR #2 feat-b -> feat-a (open) b1
PR #3 feat-c -> feat-b (open) c1
stack #4: #1 #2 #3

### github mode: merge, then sync takes in GitHub's merge and rebase
$ git stack merge
asked GitHub to merge #2 (feat-b) with the 1 below it: pending
it merges in the background; run 'git stack sync' to take in the result
[exit 0]
$ git stack sync
fetching origin
syncing stack rooted at feat-a
'feat-a': #1 merged; dropped it from the stack (the branch is kept; 'git branch -D feat-a' removes it)
'feat-b': took in origin/feat-b as GitHub left it
done.
[exit 0]
feat-a parent (dropped): 
feat-b behind origin/feat-b: 0
